04169369,-0.03534079,0.08455199,-0.04109169,0.0086034,-0.02055472,-0.00571298,0.01186859,-0.06833125,0.01454649,0.05147651,0.01550756,-0.25043634,-0.02062806,-0.04318954,0.05332159,-0.03589837,0.01714367,0.08075722,-0.00505207,-0.02392419,-0.05419574,-0.01699407,0.07187245,0.00191586,-0.04146376,0.01642931,-0.00035138,0.08440163,-0.0185578,0.03836158,-0.04786466,0.06767177,0.05371465,0.20918719,-0.0308916,0.0604156,0.04481158,-0.02058276,-0.02383449,0.0270027,-0.01349376,-0.0277303,-0.01083152,0.09273111,-0.04676277,0.02165737,0.03955085,-0.02633903,0.04318628,0.04642981,-0.04640145,-0.04784289,-0.03162944,0.00223249,0.03557451,0.11176172,0.00152462,-0.02715129,-0.0434453,0.04499751,0.03237477,-0.03930921,0.0276428,-0.03593486,-0.02391741,-0.03374005,0.05201957,-0.03013702,-0.06044068,-0.02404541,-0.03877458,0.04350939,-0.03845318,0.06931148,0.06535507,-0.05413922],"last_embed":{"tokens":380,"hash":"17ar1oa"}}},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"17ar1oa","at":1780496339142},"key":"03.Data Structure and Algorithms/79.Sudoku solver — constraint propagation + backtracking.md#79. Sudoku Solver — Constraint Propagation + Backtracking **Tier:** T2 **Parent Section:** 5. Recursion and Backtracking → 5.3 Backtracking **Links:** [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[160.Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure — the two DP conditions]] --- ## 1. Intuition First ### The Core Idea A blank Sudoku grid has 81 cells; 51 are empty on a typical puzzle. Brute force — try every digit 1–9 in every empty cell — gives 9⁵¹ ≈ 10⁴⁸ possibilities. Clearly impossible. But Sudoku is not a brute-force problem. It is a **constraint satisfaction problem (CSP)**, and the critical insight is: **most cells are not free**. Every cell belongs to three constraint groups simultaneously — its row, its column, and its 3×3 box — and each group forbids repeating any digit. A cell with only one legal digit is *not a choice at all*: it is forced. A cell with two legal digits requires a binary branch. Cells with nine legal digits are rare and appear only in nearly-empty grids. The algorithm that exploits this is a two-phase engine: 1. **Constraint Propagation:** Before guessing anything, propagate the consequences of what is already known. If cell (2,5) must be `7`, eliminate `7` from every peer of (2,5). If that elimination forces another cell to have only one legal value, propagate that too. Repeat until no more forced assignments exist. This is **arc consistency**. 2. **Backtracking Search:** When propagation reaches a dead end (multiple cells still have multiple candidates), *choose the cell with the fewest candidates* (the MRV — Minimum Remaining Values heuristic), guess one candidate, re-run propagation, and recurse. If a contradiction is found (any cell reduced to zero candidates), backtrack. ### Why This Exists Pure backtracking without propagation generates an enormous search tree. Consider: without propagation, every guess requires checking validity only *after placing the digit*. With propagation, a bad guess **immediately cascades into a contradiction** — the tree is pruned before we recurse even one level deeper. Peter Norvig's famous 2006 essay showed that with constraint propagation, most \"hard\" Sudoku puzzles are solved without any backtracking at all. The search tree for the hardest published puzzles requires fewer than 100 recursive calls — not millions. **The complexity gap:** Naive backtracking → O(9^N) where N = empty cells. With MRV + propagation → empirically O(1) on most puzzles, with worst-case still exponential for adversarially constructed inputs. The algorithmic difference is not constant-factor — it is the difference between milliseconds and heat death of the universe. ### The \"Aha\" Moment **Constraint propagation is not just pruning — it is inference.** When you eliminate a candidate from a cell, you are deducing new facts from the existing constraints without guessing anything. The backtracking search is only invoked when inference is insufficient. A well-implemented solver spends 95% of its time in propagation and 5% in search. The search is the fallback, not the core. This reframing — *solve by deduction first, search only when stuck* — is the conceptual leap that separates Sudoku's algorithm from N-Queens. N-Queens places one queen per row with no propagation; Sudoku's constraints are dense enough that propagation alone solves most instances. --- ## 2. Why We Need It — CP + ML Dual Context ### Competitive Programming **When it appears in contests:** Sudoku itself is occasionally a contest problem (usually \"solve this specific 9×9 grid\" in implementation rounds). More commonly, the *pattern* appears as: - Grid CSPs with row/column/region constraints - Exact cover problems (the mathematical generalisation of Sudoku) - Constraint-heavy assignment problems where propagation can replace search **The pattern signal:** ``` You see a grid / assignment problem where: - Each cell belongs to multiple constraint groups simultaneously - Groups enforce a \"no repeat\" or \"exactly one\" rule - The domain of each cell shrinks as others are assigned → Think: constraint propagation before backtracking ``` **Common trap:** Implementing validity checks with O(N) scans of rows/columns/boxes instead of O(1) set lookups. For 9×9 this is marginal; for 16×16 or 25×25 Sudoku variants (which appear in harder contests), this is the difference between passing and TLE. Maintain pre-allocated bitmask arrays for O(1) candidate lookup. **Second trap:** Not implementing MRV. Choosing the *next empty cell in reading order* instead of the *cell with fewest candidates* can increase the search tree by 100× on hard inputs. MRV is a one-line change with massive practical impact. ### Machine Learning / AI **1. Constraint Propagation = Belief Propagation** The propagation step in Sudoku is structurally identical to **loopy belief propagation** in graphical models. In a factor graph, each variable node (cell) sends messages to factor nodes (constraints = rows/columns/boxes) and receives back updated beliefs (candidate sets). Propagating until convergence is exactly what `scipy`'s and `pgmpy`'s belief propagation algorithms do for Markov Random Fields. Understanding Sudoku's propagation loop gives you the intuition behind BP without the probabilistic machinery. **2. MRV Heuristic = Active Learning Uncertainty Sampling** The Minimum Remaining Values heuristic — *pick the most constrained variable first* — is the CSP analog of **uncertainty sampling** in active learning. In active learning, you query the label of the sample your model is *most uncertain* about. In CSP, you branch on the variable with the *smallest domain*. Both strategies minimize wasted work by attacking the hardest decisions first. Libraries like `modAL` and `scikit-activeml` implement this principle directly. **3. Arc Consistency = Constraint Inference in Probabilistic Programming** Probabilistic programming systems like Pyro, Stan, and PyMC3 use **constraint propagation as prior tightening**. When you declare a constraint (e.g., a variable must be positive), the inference engine propagates this to tighten the prior before MCMC sampling begins. The exact same arc-consistency logic from Sudoku is applied, just in a probabilistic rather than deterministic setting. **4. Exact Cover → Dancing Links → Sparse Matrix Ops** Sudoku reduces to the **exact cover problem**, solved by Donald Knuth's Algorithm X with Dancing Links (DLX). DLX operates on a sparse binary matrix and is a form of **sparse column selection** — directly analogous to sparse linear algebra operations that underpin modern ML frameworks. `scipy.sparse` and PyTorch's sparse tensor operations use the same column-selection logic that DLX manipulates. **5. Search + Inference Interleaving (AlphaGo / AlphaZero)** AlphaGo's architecture is: neural network (fast evaluation) + MCTS (search). Sudoku's architecture is: constraint propagation (fast inference) + backtracking (search). The interleaving of *cheap inference* and *expensive search* is the same computational pattern. Understanding why Sudoku's propagation must be re-run after each guess is why you understand why AlphaZero re-evaluates board states at each MCTS node. --- ## 3. Algorithm / Pattern ### Two-Phase Architecture ``` Phase 1 — Constraint Propagation (run first, run after every assignment): For each assigned cell (r, c, digit): Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same row Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same column Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same box Naked Single rule: if any peer now has exactly 1 candidate → assign it, propagate again Hidden Single rule: if any unit (row/col/box) has exactly 1 cell where digit d can go → assign it, propagate again Contradiction detected if: any cell reduced to 0 candidates → return FAILURE Phase 2 — Backtracking Search: If all 81 cells are assigned → return SUCCESS (solution found) Select unassigned cell with fewest candidates (MRV heuristic) For each candidate digit d in that cell: Make a deep copy of the candidate state (snapshot before guess) Assign d to cell, run Phase 1 If no contradiction: recurse If recursion succeeds: return SUCCESS Else: restore snapshot (backtrack) Return FAILURE (no candidate worked) ``` ### Decision Tree for Implementation Choices ``` Is this a 9×9 standard Sudoku? ↓ yes Use bitmask-per-cell (9 bits) for O(1) candidate ops Use pre-built peer lists (20 peers per cell) Do you need one solution or all solutions? ↓ one only Add early return on first SUCCESS — don't continue search Is propagation enough? (check after full propagation pass) ↓ if all cells assigned: done, no search needed ↓ if contradiction: this puzzle is unsolvable ↓ if some cells unassigned: proceed to search Which cell to branch on? → MRV: min(cells, key=lambda c: popcount(candidates[c])) → Among ties: pick cell in most-constrained box (degree heuristic) ``` ### Complexity Analysis **Naive backtracking:** O(9^N) where N = number of empty cells. N ≤ 64 in valid puzzles → worst case 9^64 ≈ 10^61. Intractable. **With MRV + arc consistency propagation:** - Propagation cost per assignment: O(20) peers × O(1) bitmask op = O(1) amortized per cell - Number of backtracking nodes: empirically 0–100 for all published \"hard\" puzzles - Total: O(81 × 20) = O(1620) for most puzzles — effectively constant **Worst case** (adversarially crafted minimum-clue puzzles): Still exponential in theory, but in practice the hardest 17-clue puzzles solve in under 1ms with MRV + propagation. **Space:** O(81) for candidate bitmasks + O(81 × depth) for backtracking snapshots. With the standard recursion depth ≤ 81, this is O(81²) = O(6561) — trivial. --- ## 4. Pseudocode ``` // Represent state as: candidates[81], where each entry is a 9-bit mask // Bit i set → digit (i+1) is still a candidate for this cell // Cell index: r*9 + c (row-major) PEERS[cell] = precomputed list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box with cell function SOLVE(candidates): // Phase 1: propagate all forced assignments result ← PROPAGATE(candidates) if result == CONTRADICTION: return null candidates ← result // Check if solved if all cells have exactly 1 candidate: return candidates // Phase 2: MRV — pick most constrained unassigned cell cell ← argmin over unassigned cells of popcount(candidates[cell]) for each digit d where bit d is set in candidates[cell]: snapshot ← deep copy of candidates // Assign d to cell and propagate assign_result ← ASSIGN(snapshot, cell, d) if assign_result != CONTRADICTION: solution ← SOLVE(assign_result) if solution != null: return solution // Implicit backtrack: snapshot is unchanged, try next digit return null // all digits for this cell lead to contradiction function ASSIGN(candidates, cell, digit): // Remove all other candidates from this cell except digit other_digits ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared for each other_digit d in other_digits: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, d) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates function ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, digit): if digit not in candidates[cell]: return candidates // already eliminated candidates[cell] ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared // Rule 1: Naked Single — if only 1 candidate left, propagate its assignment if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 0: return CONTRADICTION // no candidates left — invalid state if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 1: forced_digit ← the remaining candidate for each peer p in PEERS[cell]: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, p, forced_digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit containing this cell for each unit U in [row_of(cell), col_of(cell), box_of(cell)]: places ← cells in U where digit is still a candidate if |places| == 0: return CONTRADICTION if |places| == 1: // Only one place for digit in this unit → assign it result ← ASSIGN(candidates, places[0], digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates ``` --- ## 5. Implementation ### C++ (Production-Ready) ```cpp #include <bits/stdc++.h> using namespace std; class SudokuSolver { // Peers: for each cell, the 20 cells sharing its row, column, or box // Units: for each cell, its 3 units (row, col, box) of 9 cells each vector<vector<int>> peers; // peers[cell] = list of 20 peer cells vector<vector<vector<int>>> units; // units[cell] = 3 groups of 9 void precompute_peers() { peers.resize(81); units.resize(81); for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int r = c / 9, col = c % 9; int box_r = (r / 3) * 3, box_c = (col / 3) * 3; set<int> peer_set; vector<int> row_unit, col_unit, box_unit; for (int i = 0; i < 9; ++i) { row_unit.push_back(r * 9 + i); col_unit.push_back(i * 9 + col); box_unit.push_back((box_r + i/3) * 9 + (box_c + i%3)); } for (auto& u : {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}) { for (int p : u) if (p != c) peer_set.insert(p); } peers[c] = vector<int>(peer_set.begin(), peer_set.end()); units[c] = {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}; } } // candidates[cell]: 9-bit mask, bit i set → digit (i+1) is candidate // Returns false if contradiction detected bool eliminate(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { if (!(cands[cell] & (1 << digit))) return true; // already eliminated cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit); int remaining = __builtin_popcount(cands[cell]); if (remaining == 0) return false; // contradiction: no candidates // Rule 1: Naked Single — propagate forced assignment if (remaining == 1) { int forced = __builtin_ctz(cands[cell]); // lowest set bit = forced digit for (int peer : peers[cell]) if (!eliminate(cands, peer, forced)) return false; } // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit for unique placement of digit for (auto& unit : units[cell]) { vector<int> places; for (int u : unit) if (cands[u] & (1 << digit)) places.push_back(u); if (places.empty()) return false; // digit can't go anywhere in unit if (places.size() == 1) if (!assign(cands, places[0], digit)) return false; } return true; } bool assign(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { // Remove all other digits from this cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) if (d != digit && (cands[cell] & (1 << d))) if (!eliminate(cands, cell, d)) return false; return true; } bool search(vector<int> cands) { // Check if solved bool solved = true; int best_cell = -1, best_count = 10; for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int cnt = __builtin_popcount(cands[c]); if (cnt == 0) return false; // contradiction if (cnt > 1) { solved = false; if (cnt < best_count) { best_count = cnt; best_cell = c; } } } if (solved) { final_state = cands; return true; } // MRV: branch on most constrained cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) { if (!(cands[best_cell] & (1 << d))) continue; vector<int> copy = cands; if (assign(copy, best_cell, d) && search(copy)) return true; } return false; } vector<int> final_state; public: SudokuSolver() { precompute_peers(); } /** * Solve Sudoku in-place. * board[i][j]: '1'-'9' for filled cells, '.' for empty * Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; exponential worst-case * Space: O(81) state + O(81 * depth) for backtracking copies */ void solveSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) { // Initialize: all 9 candidates for each cell (bits 0-8 set) vector<int> cands(81, (1 << 9) - 1); // Apply given clues for (int r = 0; r < 9; ++r) for (int c = 0; c < 9; ++c) if (board[r][c] != '.') assign(cands, r * 9 + c, board[r][c] - '1'); search(cands); // Write solution back to board for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) board[c/9][c%9] = '1' + __builtin_ctz(final_state[c]); } }; // ── Minimal test ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── int main() { // \"World's hardest Sudoku\" by Arto Inkala (2010) vector<vector<char>> board = { {'8','.','.', '.','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','3', '6','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','7','.', '.','9', '.','2','.', '.'}, {'.','5','.', '.','.', '7','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '4','5','7','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '1','.','.','.', '3'}, {'.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6','8'}, {'.','.','8', '5','.','.','.','1', '.'}, {'.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'} }; SudokuSolver solver; solver.solveSudoku(board); cout << \"Solution:\\n\"; for (auto& row : board) { for (char c : row) cout << c << ' '; cout << '\\n'; } // Expected first row: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 return 0; } ``` ### Python (Readable + Pythonic) ```python from typing import Optional from copy import deepcopy def precompute_structure() -> tuple[list[list[int]], list[list[list[int]]]]: \"\"\" Precompute peers and units for all 81 cells. peers[cell] = list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box units[cell] = list of 3 units (row, col, box), each a list of 9 cells \"\"\" peers: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] units: list[list[list[int]]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] for c in range(81): r, col = divmod(c, 9) br, bc = (r // 3) * 3, (col // 3) * 3 row_unit = [r * 9 + i for i in range(9)] col_unit = [i * 9 + col for i in range(9)] box_unit = [(br + i//3) * 9 + (bc + i%3) for i in range(9)] units[c] = [row_unit, col_unit, box_unit] peer_set = set(row_unit + col_unit + box_unit) - {c} peers[c] = list(peer_set) return peers, units # Precompute once at module level — O(81 * 27) constant work PEERS, UNITS = precompute_structure() def eliminate(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Remove `digit` (0-indexed) from candidates of `cell`. Propagates via Naked Single and Hidden Single rules. Returns False if a contradiction is detected. cands[cell]: integer bitmask, bit d set → digit d+1 is a candidate \"\"\" if not (cands[cell] & (1 << digit)): return True # already eliminated, nothing to do cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit) remaining = bin(cands[cell]).count('1') if remaining == 0: return False # contradiction: cell has no candidates # Rule 1: Naked Single — if only one candidate left, propagate it if remaining == 1: forced = (cands[cell]).bit_length() - 1 # index of the single set bit for peer in PEERS[cell]: if not eliminate(cands, peer, forced): return False # Rule 2: Hidden Single — in each unit containing this cell, # if `digit` can only go in one place, assign it there for unit in UNITS[cell]: places = [u for u in unit if cands[u] & (1 << digit)] if len(places) == 0: return False # digit has nowhere to go in this unit if len(places) == 1: if not assign(cands, places[0], digit): return False return True def assign(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Assign `digit` to `cell`: eliminate all other candidates from it. Triggers propagation via eliminate(). \"\"\" for d in range(9): if d != digit and (cands[cell] & (1 << d)): if not eliminate(cands, cell, d): return False return True def search(cands: list[int]) -> Optional[list[int]]: \"\"\" Backtracking search with MRV heuristic. Returns solved candidate array or None if no solution exists. \"\"\" # Check if solved; simultaneously find MRV cell best_cell, best_count = -1, 10 for c in range(81): cnt = bin(cands[c]).count('1') if cnt == 0: return None # contradiction if cnt > 1 and cnt < best_count: best_count, best_cell = cnt, c if best_cell == -1: return cands # all cells assigned → solved! # Branch on MRV cell: try each candidate digit for d in range(9): if not (cands[best_cell] & (1 << d)): continue copy = cands[:] # O(81) snapshot — cheap if assign(copy, best_cell, d): result = search(copy) if result is not None: return result # propagate solution up return None # all branches failed → backtrack def solve_sudoku(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: \"\"\" Solve Sudoku puzzle in-place. board[r][c]: '1'-'9' for clues, '.' for empty cells. Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; theoretically exponential worst-case Space: O(81) for candidate state; O(81 * recursion_depth) for backtracking \"\"\" # Initialise: all 9 candidates for every cell (bits 0-8 set) all_candidates = (1 << 9) - 1 # 0b111111111 cands = [all_candidates] * 81 # Apply given clues — each triggers propagation for r in range(9): for c in range(9): if board[r][c] != '.': digit = int(board[r][c]) - 1 # 0-indexed if not assign(cands, r * 9 + c, digit): raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution (contradiction in clues)\") result = search(cands) if result is None: raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution\") # Write back: single set bit in result[cell] → the solved digit for c in range(81): board[c // 9][c % 9] = str(result[c].bit_length()) # ── Visualisation helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── def print_board(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: for r, row in enumerate(board): if r in (3, 6): print(\"------+-------+------\") line = \" \".join( cell + (\" |\" if c in (2, 5) else \"\") for c, cell in enumerate(row) ) print(line) # ── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── if __name__ == \"__main__\": # Inkala's \"World's Hardest Sudoku\" (2010) inkala = [ ['8','.','.', '.','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','3', '6','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','7','.', '.','9','.', '2','.', '.'], ['.','5','.', '.','.','7', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','4','5', '7','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','1','.', '.','.', '3'], ['.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6', '8'], ['.','.','8', '5','.','.', '.','1', '.'], ['.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'], ] solve_sudoku(inkala) print(\"Inkala solution:\") print_board(inkala) # First row should be: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 # LeetCode example lc = [ ['5','3','.','.','7','.','.','.','.'], ['6','.','.','1','9','5','.','.','.'], ['.','9','8','.','.','.','.','6','.'], ['8','.','.','.','6','.','.','.','3'], ['4','.','.','8','.','3','.','.','1'], ['7','.','.','.','2','.','.','.','6'], ['.','6','.','.','.','.','2','8','.'], ['.','.','.','4','1','9','.','.','5'], ['.','.','.','.','8','.','.','7','9'], ] solve_sudoku(lc) print(\"\\nLeetCode example solution:\") print_board(lc) ``` --- ## 6. Visual Explanation ### Diagram 1: The Three Constraint Groups for Cell (4,4) ``` 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 ← columns row 0 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 1 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 2 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 3 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 4 [ C C C B X B C C C ] ← X = cell (4,4) row 5 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 6 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 7 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 8 [ . . . . R . . . . ] R = row peers (8 cells) → row_unit = {(4,0)..(4,8)} \\ {(4,4)} C = column peers (8 cells) → col_unit = {(0,4)..(8,4)} \\ {(4,4)} B = box peers (8 cells, some overlap with R/C already) → box_unit = {(3,3)..(5,5)} \\ {(4,4)} Unique peers (after dedup) = 20 cells Cell (4,4) eliminates a digit from all 20 simultaneously when assigned. ``` ### Diagram 2: Constraint Propagation in Action ``` Initial board state (partial): Row 0 partially filled. Cell (0,2) has candidates {1,3,5}. Step 1 — Assign digit 5 to cell (0,7): Propagate: eliminate 5 from all 20 peers of (0,7). → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 5. candidates become {1,3}. Step 2 — Another assignment elsewhere forces digit 3 out of row 0: → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 3. candidates become {1}. → Naked Single triggered! Only 1 candidate remains. Step 3 — Cell (0,2) is now forced: assign digit 1. → Propagate: eliminate 1 from all 20 peers of (0,2). → This may trigger further Naked/Hidden Singles in peers. This cascade — one assignment forces ten more — is why propagation alone solves most \"easy\" and \"medium\" puzzles. BEFORE propagation: 51 empty cells, 9 candidates each = up to 459 candidate-slots AFTER one round: many cells drop to 1-3 candidates. Search tree is tiny. ``` ### Diagram 3: MRV Heuristic — Why Cell Order Matters ``` Backtracking without MRV (reading order — first empty cell): Branch on cell (0,0): 4 candidates → 4 children Branch on cell (0,2): 3 candidates → 3 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 4 × 3 = 12 Backtracking with MRV (most constrained first): Branch on cell (6,5): 2 candidates → 2 children Branch on cell (2,8): 2 candidates → 2 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 2 × 2 = 4 MRV reduces branching factor from average ~3 to average ~2. Over 30 levels of recursion: 3^30 ≈ 200 billion vs 2^30 ≈ 1 billion. In practice, propagation collapses most branches before recursion, reducing depth to ~5-15 for hard puzzles. The key insight: a cell with 2 candidates is a coin flip. A cell with 5 candidates is a 5-way gamble. Attack the coin flip first — wrong guesses fail faster. ``` --- ## 7. Common Variations & Extensions ### 7.1 Larger Sudoku Variants (16×16, 25×25) Standard 9×9 Sudoku uses 9-bit masks (one int). For 16×16, extend to 16-bit masks; for 25×25, use 32-bit integers. The peer/unit precomputation generalises directly: for an N²×N² grid (N=block size), each cell has 3(N²-1) - 2(N-1) unique peers. The bitmask operations remain O(1). The key optimisation change: use `__builtin_popcount` (C++) or `bin(x).count('1')` (Python) instead of iterating candidates. ### 7.2 Sudoku Validity Checker (Not Solver) A common interview variant: \"given a partially filled board, is it valid?\" This requires only the constraint check without propagation or search — O(81) scan checking for duplicates in each row/column/box using sets. See [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] for the set-based duplicate detection pattern. ### 7.3 Exact Cover Formulation (Algorithm X / Dancing Links) Sudoku has a beautiful reduction to **exact cover**: given a universe U and a collection S of subsets, select subsets from S that partition U exactly. Sudoku's universe = {row-digit, col-digit, box-digit, cell constraints}, 324 elements. Algorithm X (Knuth 2000) with Dancing Links solves this in O(1) for most puzzles with a sparse doubly-linked list that enables O(1) \"cover\" and \"uncover\" operations. This is the fastest known exact solver. The connection to [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] is direct: DLX is constraint propagation implemented as pointer manipulation rather than bitmask ops. ### 7.4 Sudoku Generation To generate a valid puzzle: start with a solved board (easy to construct by placing 1-9 randomly in the first row and completing with backtracking), then remove clues one at a time, checking after each removal that the puzzle still has a unique solution. The uniqueness check requires running the solver and confirming the search tree has exactly one leaf. This is the algorithm used by newspaper puzzle generators. ### 7.5 Minimum Clues and Hardness The proven minimum number of clues for a uniquely-solvable 9×9 Sudoku is **17** (proved computationally in 2012 by McGuire, Tugemann, Civario). Puzzles with 17 clues are maximally hard for propagation-based solvers. Understanding *why* they're hard connects to the structure of the constraint graph — sparse clues mean few initial propagations, forcing genuine backtracking search. This is why the Inkala puzzle (above) is considered \"hard\" despite having ~20 clues. --- ## 8. LeetCode Practice Problems ``` LC: 36, 37, 51, 52, 79, 212, 980, 1307 ``` *Covers: Sudoku validity check (36), Sudoku solver (37), N-Queens I/II (51/52) for backtracking fundamentals, Word Search I/II (79/212) for grid backtracking, Unique Paths III (980) for constraint grid traversal, All O(1) Data Structure (1307) for constraint state management.* --- ## 9. CP Contest Application ### Typical Contest Structure **Direct Sudoku problems** appear occasionally in implementation rounds and ICPC regionals, typically as: - \"Solve this specific puzzle\" — pure implementation, any correct solver passes - \"Count valid completions given partial board\" — requires counting, not just finding one solution - \"Minimum clues needed for unique solution\" — research-level, not typical in CP **The constraint propagation pattern** is more broadly useful in: - **Grid CSPs:** Nonogram / Picross solving, Kakuro, Hitori — same propagation loop, different constraint definitions - **Exact cover problems:** Pentomino tiling, N-Queens counting, Latin square counting - **Assignment problems with capacity constraints:** \"Assign students to rooms such that each student gets their preference and no room is over-capacity\" — propagation reduces search dramatically ### Time Limits For 9×9 Sudoku: any correct implementation with MRV + propagation solves in < 1ms. Naive backtracking without propagation may TLE even for 9×9 on difficult inputs. For 16×16 Sudoku (occasionally appears): bitmask propagation is mandatory. Pure hash-set approach will TLE. ### Optimisation Tricks for CP 1. **Pre-allocate peer arrays at compile time** — avoid vector allocation per call: ```cpp constexpr int PEERS[81][20] = { /* precomputed at compile time */ }; ``` 2. **Use int arrays, not vectors, for candidate state** — `vector<int> copy = cands` is O(81), but `int copy[81]; memcpy(copy, cands, 81*sizeof(int))` is 3x faster due to cache effects. 3. **Skip Hidden Single rule for speed on easy puzzles** — Naked Single alone handles 95% of standard Sudoku puzzles. Add Hidden Single only if you know the test cases include hard puzzles. 4. **Iterative deepening instead of recursive backtracking** — avoids stack frame overhead on deep searches; rarely needed for 9×9 but relevant for larger variants. --- *Connections: [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] · [[128.Union-Find — union by rank and path compression]]* ENDOFFILE echo \"Done. $(wc -l < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) lines, $(wc -c < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) bytes\"``","lines":[1,9],"size":29823,"outlinks":[{"title":"128.Union-Find — union by rank and path compression","target":"128.Union-Find — union by rank and path compression","line":1},{"title":"160.Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure — the two DP conditions","target":"160.Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure — the two DP conditions","line":1},{"title":"22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns","target":"22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns","line":1},{"title":"22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns","target":"22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns","line":1},{"title":"77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose","target":"77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose","line":1},{"title":"77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose","target":"77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose","line":1},{"title":"78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem","target":"78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem","line":1},{"title":"78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem","target":"78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem","line":1},{"title":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","target":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","line":1},{"title":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","target":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","line":1},{"title":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","target":"80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions","line":1},{"title":"82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection","target":"82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection","line":1},{"title":"82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection","target":"82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection","line":1},{"title":"mes","target":"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11647753-understanding-usage-and-length-limits","line":9}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
"smart_blocks:03.Data Structure and Algorithms/79.Sudoku solver — constraint propagation + backtracking.md#79. Sudoku Solver — Constraint Propagation + Backtracking **Tier:** T2 **Parent Section:** 5. Recursion and Backtracking → 5.3 Backtracking **Links:** [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[160.Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure — the two DP conditions]] --- ## 1. Intuition First ### The Core Idea A blank Sudoku grid has 81 cells; 51 are empty on a typical puzzle. Brute force — try every digit 1–9 in every empty cell — gives 9⁵¹ ≈ 10⁴⁸ possibilities. Clearly impossible. But Sudoku is not a brute-force problem. It is a **constraint satisfaction problem (CSP)**, and the critical insight is: **most cells are not free**. Every cell belongs to three constraint groups simultaneously — its row, its column, and its 3×3 box — and each group forbids repeating any digit. A cell with only one legal digit is *not a choice at all*: it is forced. A cell with two legal digits requires a binary branch. Cells with nine legal digits are rare and appear only in nearly-empty grids. The algorithm that exploits this is a two-phase engine: 1. **Constraint Propagation:** Before guessing anything, propagate the consequences of what is already known. If cell (2,5) must be `7`, eliminate `7` from every peer of (2,5). If that elimination forces another cell to have only one legal value, propagate that too. Repeat until no more forced assignments exist. This is **arc consistency**. 2. **Backtracking Search:** When propagation reaches a dead end (multiple cells still have multiple candidates), *choose the cell with the fewest candidates* (the MRV — Minimum Remaining Values heuristic), guess one candidate, re-run propagation, and recurse. If a contradiction is found (any cell reduced to zero candidates), backtrack. ### Why This Exists Pure backtracking without propagation generates an enormous search tree. Consider: without propagation, every guess requires checking validity only *after placing the digit*. With propagation, a bad guess **immediately cascades into a contradiction** — the tree is pruned before we recurse even one level deeper. Peter Norvig's famous 2006 essay showed that with constraint propagation, most \"hard\" Sudoku puzzles are solved without any backtracking at all. The search tree for the hardest published puzzles requires fewer than 100 recursive calls — not millions. **The complexity gap:** Naive backtracking → O(9^N) where N = empty cells. With MRV + propagation → empirically O(1) on most puzzles, with worst-case still exponential for adversarially constructed inputs. The algorithmic difference is not constant-factor — it is the difference between milliseconds and heat death of the universe. ### The \"Aha\" Moment **Constraint propagation is not just pruning — it is inference.** When you eliminate a candidate from a cell, you are deducing new facts from the existing constraints without guessing anything. The backtracking search is only invoked when inference is insufficient. A well-implemented solver spends 95% of its time in propagation and 5% in search. The search is the fallback, not the core. This reframing — *solve by deduction first, search only when stuck* — is the conceptual leap that separates Sudoku's algorithm from N-Queens. N-Queens places one queen per row with no propagation; Sudoku's constraints are dense enough that propagation alone solves most instances. --- ## 2. Why We Need It — CP + ML Dual Context ### Competitive Programming **When it appears in contests:** Sudoku itself is occasionally a contest problem (usually \"solve this specific 9×9 grid\" in implementation rounds). More commonly, the *pattern* appears as: - Grid CSPs with row/column/region constraints - Exact cover problems (the mathematical generalisation of Sudoku) - Constraint-heavy assignment problems where propagation can replace search **The pattern signal:** ``` You see a grid / assignment problem where: - Each cell belongs to multiple constraint groups simultaneously - Groups enforce a \"no repeat\" or \"exactly one\" rule - The domain of each cell shrinks as others are assigned → Think: constraint propagation before backtracking ``` **Common trap:** Implementing validity checks with O(N) scans of rows/columns/boxes instead of O(1) set lookups. For 9×9 this is marginal; for 16×16 or 25×25 Sudoku variants (which appear in harder contests), this is the difference between passing and TLE. Maintain pre-allocated bitmask arrays for O(1) candidate lookup. **Second trap:** Not implementing MRV. Choosing the *next empty cell in reading order* instead of the *cell with fewest candidates* can increase the search tree by 100× on hard inputs. MRV is a one-line change with massive practical impact. ### Machine Learning / AI **1. Constraint Propagation = Belief Propagation** The propagation step in Sudoku is structurally identical to **loopy belief propagation** in graphical models. In a factor graph, each variable node (cell) sends messages to factor nodes (constraints = rows/columns/boxes) and receives back updated beliefs (candidate sets). Propagating until convergence is exactly what `scipy`'s and `pgmpy`'s belief propagation algorithms do for Markov Random Fields. Understanding Sudoku's propagation loop gives you the intuition behind BP without the probabilistic machinery. **2. MRV Heuristic = Active Learning Uncertainty Sampling** The Minimum Remaining Values heuristic — *pick the most constrained variable first* — is the CSP analog of **uncertainty sampling** in active learning. In active learning, you query the label of the sample your model is *most uncertain* about. In CSP, you branch on the variable with the *smallest domain*. Both strategies minimize wasted work by attacking the hardest decisions first. Libraries like `modAL` and `scikit-activeml` implement this principle directly. **3. Arc Consistency = Constraint Inference in Probabilistic Programming** Probabilistic programming systems like Pyro, Stan, and PyMC3 use **constraint propagation as prior tightening**. When you declare a constraint (e.g., a variable must be positive), the inference engine propagates this to tighten the prior before MCMC sampling begins. The exact same arc-consistency logic from Sudoku is applied, just in a probabilistic rather than deterministic setting. **4. Exact Cover → Dancing Links → Sparse Matrix Ops** Sudoku reduces to the **exact cover problem**, solved by Donald Knuth's Algorithm X with Dancing Links (DLX). DLX operates on a sparse binary matrix and is a form of **sparse column selection** — directly analogous to sparse linear algebra operations that underpin modern ML frameworks. `scipy.sparse` and PyTorch's sparse tensor operations use the same column-selection logic that DLX manipulates. **5. Search + Inference Interleaving (AlphaGo / AlphaZero)** AlphaGo's architecture is: neural network (fast evaluation) + MCTS (search). Sudoku's architecture is: constraint propagation (fast inference) + backtracking (search). The interleaving of *cheap inference* and *expensive search* is the same computational pattern. Understanding why Sudoku's propagation must be re-run after each guess is why you understand why AlphaZero re-evaluates board states at each MCTS node. --- ## 3. Algorithm / Pattern ### Two-Phase Architecture ``` Phase 1 — Constraint Propagation (run first, run after every assignment): For each assigned cell (r, c, digit): Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same row Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same column Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same box Naked Single rule: if any peer now has exactly 1 candidate → assign it, propagate again Hidden Single rule: if any unit (row/col/box) has exactly 1 cell where digit d can go → assign it, propagate again Contradiction detected if: any cell reduced to 0 candidates → return FAILURE Phase 2 — Backtracking Search: If all 81 cells are assigned → return SUCCESS (solution found) Select unassigned cell with fewest candidates (MRV heuristic) For each candidate digit d in that cell: Make a deep copy of the candidate state (snapshot before guess) Assign d to cell, run Phase 1 If no contradiction: recurse If recursion succeeds: return SUCCESS Else: restore snapshot (backtrack) Return FAILURE (no candidate worked) ``` ### Decision Tree for Implementation Choices ``` Is this a 9×9 standard Sudoku? ↓ yes Use bitmask-per-cell (9 bits) for O(1) candidate ops Use pre-built peer lists (20 peers per cell) Do you need one solution or all solutions? ↓ one only Add early return on first SUCCESS — don't continue search Is propagation enough? (check after full propagation pass) ↓ if all cells assigned: done, no search needed ↓ if contradiction: this puzzle is unsolvable ↓ if some cells unassigned: proceed to search Which cell to branch on? → MRV: min(cells, key=lambda c: popcount(candidates[c])) → Among ties: pick cell in most-constrained box (degree heuristic) ``` ### Complexity Analysis **Naive backtracking:** O(9^N) where N = number of empty cells. N ≤ 64 in valid puzzles → worst case 9^64 ≈ 10^61. Intractable. **With MRV + arc consistency propagation:** - Propagation cost per assignment: O(20) peers × O(1) bitmask op = O(1) amortized per cell - Number of backtracking nodes: empirically 0–100 for all published \"hard\" puzzles - Total: O(81 × 20) = O(1620) for most puzzles — effectively constant **Worst case** (adversarially crafted minimum-clue puzzles): Still exponential in theory, but in practice the hardest 17-clue puzzles solve in under 1ms with MRV + propagation. **Space:** O(81) for candidate bitmasks + O(81 × depth) for backtracking snapshots. With the standard recursion depth ≤ 81, this is O(81²) = O(6561) — trivial. --- ## 4. Pseudocode ``` // Represent state as: candidates[81], where each entry is a 9-bit mask // Bit i set → digit (i+1) is still a candidate for this cell // Cell index: r*9 + c (row-major) PEERS[cell] = precomputed list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box with cell function SOLVE(candidates): // Phase 1: propagate all forced assignments result ← PROPAGATE(candidates) if result == CONTRADICTION: return null candidates ← result // Check if solved if all cells have exactly 1 candidate: return candidates // Phase 2: MRV — pick most constrained unassigned cell cell ← argmin over unassigned cells of popcount(candidates[cell]) for each digit d where bit d is set in candidates[cell]: snapshot ← deep copy of candidates // Assign d to cell and propagate assign_result ← ASSIGN(snapshot, cell, d) if assign_result != CONTRADICTION: solution ← SOLVE(assign_result) if solution != null: return solution // Implicit backtrack: snapshot is unchanged, try next digit return null // all digits for this cell lead to contradiction function ASSIGN(candidates, cell, digit): // Remove all other candidates from this cell except digit other_digits ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared for each other_digit d in other_digits: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, d) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates function ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, digit): if digit not in candidates[cell]: return candidates // already eliminated candidates[cell] ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared // Rule 1: Naked Single — if only 1 candidate left, propagate its assignment if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 0: return CONTRADICTION // no candidates left — invalid state if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 1: forced_digit ← the remaining candidate for each peer p in PEERS[cell]: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, p, forced_digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit containing this cell for each unit U in [row_of(cell), col_of(cell), box_of(cell)]: places ← cells in U where digit is still a candidate if |places| == 0: return CONTRADICTION if |places| == 1: // Only one place for digit in this unit → assign it result ← ASSIGN(candidates, places[0], digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates ``` --- ## 5. Implementation ### C++ (Production-Ready) ```cpp #include <bits/stdc++.h> using namespace std; class SudokuSolver { // Peers: for each cell, the 20 cells sharing its row, column, or box // Units: for each cell, its 3 units (row, col, box) of 9 cells each vector<vector<int>> peers; // peers[cell] = list of 20 peer cells vector<vector<vector<int>>> units; // units[cell] = 3 groups of 9 void precompute_peers() { peers.resize(81); units.resize(81); for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int r = c / 9, col = c % 9; int box_r = (r / 3) * 3, box_c = (col / 3) * 3; set<int> peer_set; vector<int> row_unit, col_unit, box_unit; for (int i = 0; i < 9; ++i) { row_unit.push_back(r * 9 + i); col_unit.push_back(i * 9 + col); box_unit.push_back((box_r + i/3) * 9 + (box_c + i%3)); } for (auto& u : {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}) { for (int p : u) if (p != c) peer_set.insert(p); } peers[c] = vector<int>(peer_set.begin(), peer_set.end()); units[c] = {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}; } } // candidates[cell]: 9-bit mask, bit i set → digit (i+1) is candidate // Returns false if contradiction detected bool eliminate(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { if (!(cands[cell] & (1 << digit))) return true; // already eliminated cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit); int remaining = __builtin_popcount(cands[cell]); if (remaining == 0) return false; // contradiction: no candidates // Rule 1: Naked Single — propagate forced assignment if (remaining == 1) { int forced = __builtin_ctz(cands[cell]); // lowest set bit = forced digit for (int peer : peers[cell]) if (!eliminate(cands, peer, forced)) return false; } // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit for unique placement of digit for (auto& unit : units[cell]) { vector<int> places; for (int u : unit) if (cands[u] & (1 << digit)) places.push_back(u); if (places.empty()) return false; // digit can't go anywhere in unit if (places.size() == 1) if (!assign(cands, places[0], digit)) return false; } return true; } bool assign(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { // Remove all other digits from this cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) if (d != digit && (cands[cell] & (1 << d))) if (!eliminate(cands, cell, d)) return false; return true; } bool search(vector<int> cands) { // Check if solved bool solved = true; int best_cell = -1, best_count = 10; for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int cnt = __builtin_popcount(cands[c]); if (cnt == 0) return false; // contradiction if (cnt > 1) { solved = false; if (cnt < best_count) { best_count = cnt; best_cell = c; } } } if (solved) { final_state = cands; return true; } // MRV: branch on most constrained cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) { if (!(cands[best_cell] & (1 << d))) continue; vector<int> copy = cands; if (assign(copy, best_cell, d) && search(copy)) return true; } return false; } vector<int> final_state; public: SudokuSolver() { precompute_peers(); } /** * Solve Sudoku in-place. * board[i][j]: '1'-'9' for filled cells, '.' for empty * Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; exponential worst-case * Space: O(81) state + O(81 * depth) for backtracking copies */ void solveSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) { // Initialize: all 9 candidates for each cell (bits 0-8 set) vector<int> cands(81, (1 << 9) - 1); // Apply given clues for (int r = 0; r < 9; ++r) for (int c = 0; c < 9; ++c) if (board[r][c] != '.') assign(cands, r * 9 + c, board[r][c] - '1'); search(cands); // Write solution back to board for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) board[c/9][c%9] = '1' + __builtin_ctz(final_state[c]); } }; // ── Minimal test ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── int main() { // \"World's hardest Sudoku\" by Arto Inkala (2010) vector<vector<char>> board = { {'8','.','.', '.','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','3', '6','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','7','.', '.','9', '.','2','.', '.'}, {'.','5','.', '.','.', '7','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '4','5','7','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '1','.','.','.', '3'}, {'.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6','8'}, {'.','.','8', '5','.','.','.','1', '.'}, {'.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'} }; SudokuSolver solver; solver.solveSudoku(board); cout << \"Solution:\\n\"; for (auto& row : board) { for (char c : row) cout << c << ' '; cout << '\\n'; } // Expected first row: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 return 0; } ``` ### Python (Readable + Pythonic) ```python from typing import Optional from copy import deepcopy def precompute_structure() -> tuple[list[list[int]], list[list[list[int]]]]: \"\"\" Precompute peers and units for all 81 cells. peers[cell] = list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box units[cell] = list of 3 units (row, col, box), each a list of 9 cells \"\"\" peers: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] units: list[list[list[int]]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] for c in range(81): r, col = divmod(c, 9) br, bc = (r // 3) * 3, (col // 3) * 3 row_unit = [r * 9 + i for i in range(9)] col_unit = [i * 9 + col for i in range(9)] box_unit = [(br + i//3) * 9 + (bc + i%3) for i in range(9)] units[c] = [row_unit, col_unit, box_unit] peer_set = set(row_unit + col_unit + box_unit) - {c} peers[c] = list(peer_set) return peers, units # Precompute once at module level — O(81 * 27) constant work PEERS, UNITS = precompute_structure() def eliminate(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Remove `digit` (0-indexed) from candidates of `cell`. Propagates via Naked Single and Hidden Single rules. Returns False if a contradiction is detected. cands[cell]: integer bitmask, bit d set → digit d+1 is a candidate \"\"\" if not (cands[cell] & (1 << digit)): return True # already eliminated, nothing to do cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit) remaining = bin(cands[cell]).count('1') if remaining == 0: return False # contradiction: cell has no candidates # Rule 1: Naked Single — if only one candidate left, propagate it if remaining == 1: forced = (cands[cell]).bit_length() - 1 # index of the single set bit for peer in PEERS[cell]: if not eliminate(cands, peer, forced): return False # Rule 2: Hidden Single — in each unit containing this cell, # if `digit` can only go in one place, assign it there for unit in UNITS[cell]: places = [u for u in unit if cands[u] & (1 << digit)] if len(places) == 0: return False # digit has nowhere to go in this unit if len(places) == 1: if not assign(cands, places[0], digit): return False return True def assign(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Assign `digit` to `cell`: eliminate all other candidates from it. Triggers propagation via eliminate(). \"\"\" for d in range(9): if d != digit and (cands[cell] & (1 << d)): if not eliminate(cands, cell, d): return False return True def search(cands: list[int]) -> Optional[list[int]]: \"\"\" Backtracking search with MRV heuristic. Returns solved candidate array or None if no solution exists. \"\"\" # Check if solved; simultaneously find MRV cell best_cell, best_count = -1, 10 for c in range(81): cnt = bin(cands[c]).count('1') if cnt == 0: return None # contradiction if cnt > 1 and cnt < best_count: best_count, best_cell = cnt, c if best_cell == -1: return cands # all cells assigned → solved! # Branch on MRV cell: try each candidate digit for d in range(9): if not (cands[best_cell] & (1 << d)): continue copy = cands[:] # O(81) snapshot — cheap if assign(copy, best_cell, d): result = search(copy) if result is not None: return result # propagate solution up return None # all branches failed → backtrack def solve_sudoku(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: \"\"\" Solve Sudoku puzzle in-place. board[r][c]: '1'-'9' for clues, '.' for empty cells. Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; theoretically exponential worst-case Space: O(81) for candidate state; O(81 * recursion_depth) for backtracking \"\"\" # Initialise: all 9 candidates for every cell (bits 0-8 set) all_candidates = (1 << 9) - 1 # 0b111111111 cands = [all_candidates] * 81 # Apply given clues — each triggers propagation for r in range(9): for c in range(9): if board[r][c] != '.': digit = int(board[r][c]) - 1 # 0-indexed if not assign(cands, r * 9 + c, digit): raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution (contradiction in clues)\") result = search(cands) if result is None: raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution\") # Write back: single set bit in result[cell] → the solved digit for c in range(81): board[c // 9][c % 9] = str(result[c].bit_length()) # ── Visualisation helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── def print_board(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: for r, row in enumerate(board): if r in (3, 6): print(\"------+-------+------\") line = \" \".join( cell + (\" |\" if c in (2, 5) else \"\") for c, cell in enumerate(row) ) print(line) # ── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── if __name__ == \"__main__\": # Inkala's \"World's Hardest Sudoku\" (2010) inkala = [ ['8','.','.', '.','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','3', '6','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','7','.', '.','9','.', '2','.', '.'], ['.','5','.', '.','.','7', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','4','5', '7','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','1','.', '.','.', '3'], ['.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6', '8'], ['.','.','8', '5','.','.', '.','1', '.'], ['.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'], ] solve_sudoku(inkala) print(\"Inkala solution:\") print_board(inkala) # First row should be: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 # LeetCode example lc = [ ['5','3','.','.','7','.','.','.','.'], ['6','.','.','1','9','5','.','.','.'], ['.','9','8','.','.','.','.','6','.'], ['8','.','.','.','6','.','.','.','3'], ['4','.','.','8','.','3','.','.','1'], ['7','.','.','.','2','.','.','.','6'], ['.','6','.','.','.','.','2','8','.'], ['.','.','.','4','1','9','.','.','5'], ['.','.','.','.','8','.','.','7','9'], ] solve_sudoku(lc) print(\"\\nLeetCode example solution:\") print_board(lc) ``` --- ## 6. Visual Explanation ### Diagram 1: The Three Constraint Groups for Cell (4,4) ``` 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 ← columns row 0 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 1 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 2 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 3 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 4 [ C C C B X B C C C ] ← X = cell (4,4) row 5 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 6 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 7 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 8 [ . . . . R . . . . ] R = row peers (8 cells) → row_unit = {(4,0)..(4,8)} \\ {(4,4)} C = column peers (8 cells) → col_unit = {(0,4)..(8,4)} \\ {(4,4)} B = box peers (8 cells, some overlap with R/C already) → box_unit = {(3,3)..(5,5)} \\ {(4,4)} Unique peers (after dedup) = 20 cells Cell (4,4) eliminates a digit from all 20 simultaneously when assigned. ``` ### Diagram 2: Constraint Propagation in Action ``` Initial board state (partial): Row 0 partially filled. Cell (0,2) has candidates {1,3,5}. Step 1 — Assign digit 5 to cell (0,7): Propagate: eliminate 5 from all 20 peers of (0,7). → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 5. candidates become {1,3}. Step 2 — Another assignment elsewhere forces digit 3 out of row 0: → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 3. candidates become {1}. → Naked Single triggered! Only 1 candidate remains. Step 3 — Cell (0,2) is now forced: assign digit 1. → Propagate: eliminate 1 from all 20 peers of (0,2). → This may trigger further Naked/Hidden Singles in peers. This cascade — one assignment forces ten more — is why propagation alone solves most \"easy\" and \"medium\" puzzles. BEFORE propagation: 51 empty cells, 9 candidates each = up to 459 candidate-slots AFTER one round: many cells drop to 1-3 candidates. Search tree is tiny. ``` ### Diagram 3: MRV Heuristic — Why Cell Order Matters ``` Backtracking without MRV (reading order — first empty cell): Branch on cell (0,0): 4 candidates → 4 children Branch on cell (0,2): 3 candidates → 3 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 4 × 3 = 12 Backtracking with MRV (most constrained first): Branch on cell (6,5): 2 candidates → 2 children Branch on cell (2,8): 2 candidates → 2 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 2 × 2 = 4 MRV reduces branching factor from average ~3 to average ~2. Over 30 levels of recursion: 3^30 ≈ 200 billion vs 2^30 ≈ 1 billion. In practice, propagation collapses most branches before recursion, reducing depth to ~5-15 for hard puzzles. The key insight: a cell with 2 candidates is a coin flip. A cell with 5 candidates is a 5-way gamble. Attack the coin flip first — wrong guesses fail faster. ``` --- ## 7. Common Variations & Extensions ### 7.1 Larger Sudoku Variants (16×16, 25×25) Standard 9×9 Sudoku uses 9-bit masks (one int). For 16×16, extend to 16-bit masks; for 25×25, use 32-bit integers. The peer/unit precomputation generalises directly: for an N²×N² grid (N=block size), each cell has 3(N²-1) - 2(N-1) unique peers. The bitmask operations remain O(1). The key optimisation change: use `__builtin_popcount` (C++) or `bin(x).count('1')` (Python) instead of iterating candidates. ### 7.2 Sudoku Validity Checker (Not Solver) A common interview variant: \"given a partially filled board, is it valid?\" This requires only the constraint check without propagation or search — O(81) scan checking for duplicates in each row/column/box using sets. See [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] for the set-based duplicate detection pattern. ### 7.3 Exact Cover Formulation (Algorithm X / Dancing Links) Sudoku has a beautiful reduction to **exact cover**: given a universe U and a collection S of subsets, select subsets from S that partition U exactly. Sudoku's universe = {row-digit, col-digit, box-digit, cell constraints}, 324 elements. Algorithm X (Knuth 2000) with Dancing Links solves this in O(1) for most puzzles with a sparse doubly-linked list that enables O(1) \"cover\" and \"uncover\" operations. This is the fastest known exact solver. The connection to [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] is direct: DLX is constraint propagation implemented as pointer manipulation rather than bitmask ops. ### 7.4 Sudoku Generation To generate a valid puzzle: start with a solved board (easy to construct by placing 1-9 randomly in the first row and completing with backtracking), then remove clues one at a time, checking after each removal that the puzzle still has a unique solution. The uniqueness check requires running the solver and confirming the search tree has exactly one leaf. This is the algorithm used by newspaper puzzle generators. ### 7.5 Minimum Clues and Hardness The proven minimum number of clues for a uniquely-solvable 9×9 Sudoku is **17** (proved computationally in 2012 by McGuire, Tugemann, Civario). Puzzles with 17 clues are maximally hard for propagation-based solvers. Understanding *why* they're hard connects to the structure of the constraint graph — sparse clues mean few initial propagations, forcing genuine backtracking search. This is why the Inkala puzzle (above) is considered \"hard\" despite having ~20 clues. --- ## 8. LeetCode Practice Problems ``` LC: 36, 37, 51, 52, 79, 212, 980, 1307 ``` *Covers: Sudoku validity check (36), Sudoku solver (37), N-Queens I/II (51/52) for backtracking fundamentals, Word Search I/II (79/212) for grid backtracking, Unique Paths III (980) for constraint grid traversal, All O(1) Data Structure (1307) for constraint state management.* --- ## 9. CP Contest Application ### Typical Contest Structure **Direct Sudoku problems** appear occasionally in implementation rounds and ICPC regionals, typically as: - \"Solve this specific puzzle\" — pure implementation, any correct solver passes - \"Count valid completions given partial board\" — requires counting, not just finding one solution - \"Minimum clues needed for unique solution\" — research-level, not typical in CP **The constraint propagation pattern** is more broadly useful in: - **Grid CSPs:** Nonogram / Picross solving, Kakuro, Hitori — same propagation loop, different constraint definitions - **Exact cover problems:** Pentomino tiling, N-Queens counting, Latin square counting - **Assignment problems with capacity constraints:** \"Assign students to rooms such that each student gets their preference and no room is over-capacity\" — propagation reduces search dramatically ### Time Limits For 9×9 Sudoku: any correct implementation with MRV + propagation solves in < 1ms. Naive backtracking without propagation may TLE even for 9×9 on difficult inputs. For 16×16 Sudoku (occasionally appears): bitmask propagation is mandatory. Pure hash-set approach will TLE. ### Optimisation Tricks for CP 1. **Pre-allocate peer arrays at compile time** — avoid vector allocation per call: ```cpp constexpr int PEERS[81][20] = { /* precomputed at compile time */ }; ``` 2. **Use int arrays, not vectors, for candidate state** — `vector<int> copy = cands` is O(81), but `int copy[81]; memcpy(copy, cands, 81*sizeof(int))` is 3x faster due to cache effects. 3. **Skip Hidden Single rule for speed on easy puzzles** — Naked Single alone handles 95% of standard Sudoku puzzles. Add Hidden Single only if you know the test cases include hard puzzles. 4. **Iterative deepening instead of recursive backtracking** — avoids stack frame overhead on deep searches; rarely needed for 9×9 but relevant for larger variants. --- *Connections: [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] · [[128.Union-Find — union by rank and path compression]]* ENDOFFILE echo \"Done. $(wc -l < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) lines, $(wc -c < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) bytes\"``#{1}": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"1x9jm9l","at":1780496338947},"key":"03.Data Structure and Algorithms/79.Sudoku solver — constraint propagation + backtracking.md#79. Sudoku Solver — Constraint Propagation + Backtracking **Tier:** T2 **Parent Section:** 5. Recursion and Backtracking → 5.3 Backtracking **Links:** [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[160.Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure — the two DP conditions]] --- ## 1. Intuition First ### The Core Idea A blank Sudoku grid has 81 cells; 51 are empty on a typical puzzle. Brute force — try every digit 1–9 in every empty cell — gives 9⁵¹ ≈ 10⁴⁸ possibilities. Clearly impossible. But Sudoku is not a brute-force problem. It is a **constraint satisfaction problem (CSP)**, and the critical insight is: **most cells are not free**. Every cell belongs to three constraint groups simultaneously — its row, its column, and its 3×3 box — and each group forbids repeating any digit. A cell with only one legal digit is *not a choice at all*: it is forced. A cell with two legal digits requires a binary branch. Cells with nine legal digits are rare and appear only in nearly-empty grids. The algorithm that exploits this is a two-phase engine: 1. **Constraint Propagation:** Before guessing anything, propagate the consequences of what is already known. If cell (2,5) must be `7`, eliminate `7` from every peer of (2,5). If that elimination forces another cell to have only one legal value, propagate that too. Repeat until no more forced assignments exist. This is **arc consistency**. 2. **Backtracking Search:** When propagation reaches a dead end (multiple cells still have multiple candidates), *choose the cell with the fewest candidates* (the MRV — Minimum Remaining Values heuristic), guess one candidate, re-run propagation, and recurse. If a contradiction is found (any cell reduced to zero candidates), backtrack. ### Why This Exists Pure backtracking without propagation generates an enormous search tree. Consider: without propagation, every guess requires checking validity only *after placing the digit*. With propagation, a bad guess **immediately cascades into a contradiction** — the tree is pruned before we recurse even one level deeper. Peter Norvig's famous 2006 essay showed that with constraint propagation, most \"hard\" Sudoku puzzles are solved without any backtracking at all. The search tree for the hardest published puzzles requires fewer than 100 recursive calls — not millions. **The complexity gap:** Naive backtracking → O(9^N) where N = empty cells. With MRV + propagation → empirically O(1) on most puzzles, with worst-case still exponential for adversarially constructed inputs. The algorithmic difference is not constant-factor — it is the difference between milliseconds and heat death of the universe. ### The \"Aha\" Moment **Constraint propagation is not just pruning — it is inference.** When you eliminate a candidate from a cell, you are deducing new facts from the existing constraints without guessing anything. The backtracking search is only invoked when inference is insufficient. A well-implemented solver spends 95% of its time in propagation and 5% in search. The search is the fallback, not the core. This reframing — *solve by deduction first, search only when stuck* — is the conceptual leap that separates Sudoku's algorithm from N-Queens. N-Queens places one queen per row with no propagation; Sudoku's constraints are dense enough that propagation alone solves most instances. --- ## 2. Why We Need It — CP + ML Dual Context ### Competitive Programming **When it appears in contests:** Sudoku itself is occasionally a contest problem (usually \"solve this specific 9×9 grid\" in implementation rounds). More commonly, the *pattern* appears as: - Grid CSPs with row/column/region constraints - Exact cover problems (the mathematical generalisation of Sudoku) - Constraint-heavy assignment problems where propagation can replace search **The pattern signal:** ``` You see a grid / assignment problem where: - Each cell belongs to multiple constraint groups simultaneously - Groups enforce a \"no repeat\" or \"exactly one\" rule - The domain of each cell shrinks as others are assigned → Think: constraint propagation before backtracking ``` **Common trap:** Implementing validity checks with O(N) scans of rows/columns/boxes instead of O(1) set lookups. For 9×9 this is marginal; for 16×16 or 25×25 Sudoku variants (which appear in harder contests), this is the difference between passing and TLE. Maintain pre-allocated bitmask arrays for O(1) candidate lookup. **Second trap:** Not implementing MRV. Choosing the *next empty cell in reading order* instead of the *cell with fewest candidates* can increase the search tree by 100× on hard inputs. MRV is a one-line change with massive practical impact. ### Machine Learning / AI **1. Constraint Propagation = Belief Propagation** The propagation step in Sudoku is structurally identical to **loopy belief propagation** in graphical models. In a factor graph, each variable node (cell) sends messages to factor nodes (constraints = rows/columns/boxes) and receives back updated beliefs (candidate sets). Propagating until convergence is exactly what `scipy`'s and `pgmpy`'s belief propagation algorithms do for Markov Random Fields. Understanding Sudoku's propagation loop gives you the intuition behind BP without the probabilistic machinery. **2. MRV Heuristic = Active Learning Uncertainty Sampling** The Minimum Remaining Values heuristic — *pick the most constrained variable first* — is the CSP analog of **uncertainty sampling** in active learning. In active learning, you query the label of the sample your model is *most uncertain* about. In CSP, you branch on the variable with the *smallest domain*. Both strategies minimize wasted work by attacking the hardest decisions first. Libraries like `modAL` and `scikit-activeml` implement this principle directly. **3. Arc Consistency = Constraint Inference in Probabilistic Programming** Probabilistic programming systems like Pyro, Stan, and PyMC3 use **constraint propagation as prior tightening**. When you declare a constraint (e.g., a variable must be positive), the inference engine propagates this to tighten the prior before MCMC sampling begins. The exact same arc-consistency logic from Sudoku is applied, just in a probabilistic rather than deterministic setting. **4. Exact Cover → Dancing Links → Sparse Matrix Ops** Sudoku reduces to the **exact cover problem**, solved by Donald Knuth's Algorithm X with Dancing Links (DLX). DLX operates on a sparse binary matrix and is a form of **sparse column selection** — directly analogous to sparse linear algebra operations that underpin modern ML frameworks. `scipy.sparse` and PyTorch's sparse tensor operations use the same column-selection logic that DLX manipulates. **5. Search + Inference Interleaving (AlphaGo / AlphaZero)** AlphaGo's architecture is: neural network (fast evaluation) + MCTS (search). Sudoku's architecture is: constraint propagation (fast inference) + backtracking (search). The interleaving of *cheap inference* and *expensive search* is the same computational pattern. Understanding why Sudoku's propagation must be re-run after each guess is why you understand why AlphaZero re-evaluates board states at each MCTS node. --- ## 3. Algorithm / Pattern ### Two-Phase Architecture ``` Phase 1 — Constraint Propagation (run first, run after every assignment): For each assigned cell (r, c, digit): Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same row Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same column Remove digit from candidates of all peers in same box Naked Single rule: if any peer now has exactly 1 candidate → assign it, propagate again Hidden Single rule: if any unit (row/col/box) has exactly 1 cell where digit d can go → assign it, propagate again Contradiction detected if: any cell reduced to 0 candidates → return FAILURE Phase 2 — Backtracking Search: If all 81 cells are assigned → return SUCCESS (solution found) Select unassigned cell with fewest candidates (MRV heuristic) For each candidate digit d in that cell: Make a deep copy of the candidate state (snapshot before guess) Assign d to cell, run Phase 1 If no contradiction: recurse If recursion succeeds: return SUCCESS Else: restore snapshot (backtrack) Return FAILURE (no candidate worked) ``` ### Decision Tree for Implementation Choices ``` Is this a 9×9 standard Sudoku? ↓ yes Use bitmask-per-cell (9 bits) for O(1) candidate ops Use pre-built peer lists (20 peers per cell) Do you need one solution or all solutions? ↓ one only Add early return on first SUCCESS — don't continue search Is propagation enough? (check after full propagation pass) ↓ if all cells assigned: done, no search needed ↓ if contradiction: this puzzle is unsolvable ↓ if some cells unassigned: proceed to search Which cell to branch on? → MRV: min(cells, key=lambda c: popcount(candidates[c])) → Among ties: pick cell in most-constrained box (degree heuristic) ``` ### Complexity Analysis **Naive backtracking:** O(9^N) where N = number of empty cells. N ≤ 64 in valid puzzles → worst case 9^64 ≈ 10^61. Intractable. **With MRV + arc consistency propagation:** - Propagation cost per assignment: O(20) peers × O(1) bitmask op = O(1) amortized per cell - Number of backtracking nodes: empirically 0–100 for all published \"hard\" puzzles - Total: O(81 × 20) = O(1620) for most puzzles — effectively constant **Worst case** (adversarially crafted minimum-clue puzzles): Still exponential in theory, but in practice the hardest 17-clue puzzles solve in under 1ms with MRV + propagation. **Space:** O(81) for candidate bitmasks + O(81 × depth) for backtracking snapshots. With the standard recursion depth ≤ 81, this is O(81²) = O(6561) — trivial. --- ## 4. Pseudocode ``` // Represent state as: candidates[81], where each entry is a 9-bit mask // Bit i set → digit (i+1) is still a candidate for this cell // Cell index: r*9 + c (row-major) PEERS[cell] = precomputed list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box with cell function SOLVE(candidates): // Phase 1: propagate all forced assignments result ← PROPAGATE(candidates) if result == CONTRADICTION: return null candidates ← result // Check if solved if all cells have exactly 1 candidate: return candidates // Phase 2: MRV — pick most constrained unassigned cell cell ← argmin over unassigned cells of popcount(candidates[cell]) for each digit d where bit d is set in candidates[cell]: snapshot ← deep copy of candidates // Assign d to cell and propagate assign_result ← ASSIGN(snapshot, cell, d) if assign_result != CONTRADICTION: solution ← SOLVE(assign_result) if solution != null: return solution // Implicit backtrack: snapshot is unchanged, try next digit return null // all digits for this cell lead to contradiction function ASSIGN(candidates, cell, digit): // Remove all other candidates from this cell except digit other_digits ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared for each other_digit d in other_digits: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, d) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates function ELIMINATE(candidates, cell, digit): if digit not in candidates[cell]: return candidates // already eliminated candidates[cell] ← candidates[cell] with bit (digit) cleared // Rule 1: Naked Single — if only 1 candidate left, propagate its assignment if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 0: return CONTRADICTION // no candidates left — invalid state if popcount(candidates[cell]) == 1: forced_digit ← the remaining candidate for each peer p in PEERS[cell]: result ← ELIMINATE(candidates, p, forced_digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit containing this cell for each unit U in [row_of(cell), col_of(cell), box_of(cell)]: places ← cells in U where digit is still a candidate if |places| == 0: return CONTRADICTION if |places| == 1: // Only one place for digit in this unit → assign it result ← ASSIGN(candidates, places[0], digit) if result == CONTRADICTION: return CONTRADICTION return candidates ``` --- ## 5. Implementation ### C++ (Production-Ready) ```cpp #include <bits/stdc++.h> using namespace std; class SudokuSolver { // Peers: for each cell, the 20 cells sharing its row, column, or box // Units: for each cell, its 3 units (row, col, box) of 9 cells each vector<vector<int>> peers; // peers[cell] = list of 20 peer cells vector<vector<vector<int>>> units; // units[cell] = 3 groups of 9 void precompute_peers() { peers.resize(81); units.resize(81); for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int r = c / 9, col = c % 9; int box_r = (r / 3) * 3, box_c = (col / 3) * 3; set<int> peer_set; vector<int> row_unit, col_unit, box_unit; for (int i = 0; i < 9; ++i) { row_unit.push_back(r * 9 + i); col_unit.push_back(i * 9 + col); box_unit.push_back((box_r + i/3) * 9 + (box_c + i%3)); } for (auto& u : {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}) { for (int p : u) if (p != c) peer_set.insert(p); } peers[c] = vector<int>(peer_set.begin(), peer_set.end()); units[c] = {row_unit, col_unit, box_unit}; } } // candidates[cell]: 9-bit mask, bit i set → digit (i+1) is candidate // Returns false if contradiction detected bool eliminate(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { if (!(cands[cell] & (1 << digit))) return true; // already eliminated cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit); int remaining = __builtin_popcount(cands[cell]); if (remaining == 0) return false; // contradiction: no candidates // Rule 1: Naked Single — propagate forced assignment if (remaining == 1) { int forced = __builtin_ctz(cands[cell]); // lowest set bit = forced digit for (int peer : peers[cell]) if (!eliminate(cands, peer, forced)) return false; } // Rule 2: Hidden Single — check each unit for unique placement of digit for (auto& unit : units[cell]) { vector<int> places; for (int u : unit) if (cands[u] & (1 << digit)) places.push_back(u); if (places.empty()) return false; // digit can't go anywhere in unit if (places.size() == 1) if (!assign(cands, places[0], digit)) return false; } return true; } bool assign(vector<int>& cands, int cell, int digit) { // Remove all other digits from this cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) if (d != digit && (cands[cell] & (1 << d))) if (!eliminate(cands, cell, d)) return false; return true; } bool search(vector<int> cands) { // Check if solved bool solved = true; int best_cell = -1, best_count = 10; for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) { int cnt = __builtin_popcount(cands[c]); if (cnt == 0) return false; // contradiction if (cnt > 1) { solved = false; if (cnt < best_count) { best_count = cnt; best_cell = c; } } } if (solved) { final_state = cands; return true; } // MRV: branch on most constrained cell for (int d = 0; d < 9; ++d) { if (!(cands[best_cell] & (1 << d))) continue; vector<int> copy = cands; if (assign(copy, best_cell, d) && search(copy)) return true; } return false; } vector<int> final_state; public: SudokuSolver() { precompute_peers(); } /** * Solve Sudoku in-place. * board[i][j]: '1'-'9' for filled cells, '.' for empty * Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; exponential worst-case * Space: O(81) state + O(81 * depth) for backtracking copies */ void solveSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) { // Initialize: all 9 candidates for each cell (bits 0-8 set) vector<int> cands(81, (1 << 9) - 1); // Apply given clues for (int r = 0; r < 9; ++r) for (int c = 0; c < 9; ++c) if (board[r][c] != '.') assign(cands, r * 9 + c, board[r][c] - '1'); search(cands); // Write solution back to board for (int c = 0; c < 81; ++c) board[c/9][c%9] = '1' + __builtin_ctz(final_state[c]); } }; // ── Minimal test ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── int main() { // \"World's hardest Sudoku\" by Arto Inkala (2010) vector<vector<char>> board = { {'8','.','.', '.','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','3', '6','.', '.','.','.', '.'}, {'.','7','.', '.','9', '.','2','.', '.'}, {'.','5','.', '.','.', '7','.','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '4','5','7','.', '.'}, {'.','.','.','.', '1','.','.','.', '3'}, {'.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6','8'}, {'.','.','8', '5','.','.','.','1', '.'}, {'.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'} }; SudokuSolver solver; solver.solveSudoku(board); cout << \"Solution:\\n\"; for (auto& row : board) { for (char c : row) cout << c << ' '; cout << '\\n'; } // Expected first row: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 return 0; } ``` ### Python (Readable + Pythonic) ```python from typing import Optional from copy import deepcopy def precompute_structure() -> tuple[list[list[int]], list[list[list[int]]]]: \"\"\" Precompute peers and units for all 81 cells. peers[cell] = list of 20 cells sharing row/col/box units[cell] = list of 3 units (row, col, box), each a list of 9 cells \"\"\" peers: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] units: list[list[list[int]]] = [[] for _ in range(81)] for c in range(81): r, col = divmod(c, 9) br, bc = (r // 3) * 3, (col // 3) * 3 row_unit = [r * 9 + i for i in range(9)] col_unit = [i * 9 + col for i in range(9)] box_unit = [(br + i//3) * 9 + (bc + i%3) for i in range(9)] units[c] = [row_unit, col_unit, box_unit] peer_set = set(row_unit + col_unit + box_unit) - {c} peers[c] = list(peer_set) return peers, units # Precompute once at module level — O(81 * 27) constant work PEERS, UNITS = precompute_structure() def eliminate(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Remove `digit` (0-indexed) from candidates of `cell`. Propagates via Naked Single and Hidden Single rules. Returns False if a contradiction is detected. cands[cell]: integer bitmask, bit d set → digit d+1 is a candidate \"\"\" if not (cands[cell] & (1 << digit)): return True # already eliminated, nothing to do cands[cell] &= ~(1 << digit) remaining = bin(cands[cell]).count('1') if remaining == 0: return False # contradiction: cell has no candidates # Rule 1: Naked Single — if only one candidate left, propagate it if remaining == 1: forced = (cands[cell]).bit_length() - 1 # index of the single set bit for peer in PEERS[cell]: if not eliminate(cands, peer, forced): return False # Rule 2: Hidden Single — in each unit containing this cell, # if `digit` can only go in one place, assign it there for unit in UNITS[cell]: places = [u for u in unit if cands[u] & (1 << digit)] if len(places) == 0: return False # digit has nowhere to go in this unit if len(places) == 1: if not assign(cands, places[0], digit): return False return True def assign(cands: list[int], cell: int, digit: int) -> bool: \"\"\" Assign `digit` to `cell`: eliminate all other candidates from it. Triggers propagation via eliminate(). \"\"\" for d in range(9): if d != digit and (cands[cell] & (1 << d)): if not eliminate(cands, cell, d): return False return True def search(cands: list[int]) -> Optional[list[int]]: \"\"\" Backtracking search with MRV heuristic. Returns solved candidate array or None if no solution exists. \"\"\" # Check if solved; simultaneously find MRV cell best_cell, best_count = -1, 10 for c in range(81): cnt = bin(cands[c]).count('1') if cnt == 0: return None # contradiction if cnt > 1 and cnt < best_count: best_count, best_cell = cnt, c if best_cell == -1: return cands # all cells assigned → solved! # Branch on MRV cell: try each candidate digit for d in range(9): if not (cands[best_cell] & (1 << d)): continue copy = cands[:] # O(81) snapshot — cheap if assign(copy, best_cell, d): result = search(copy) if result is not None: return result # propagate solution up return None # all branches failed → backtrack def solve_sudoku(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: \"\"\" Solve Sudoku puzzle in-place. board[r][c]: '1'-'9' for clues, '.' for empty cells. Time: O(1) empirically on valid puzzles; theoretically exponential worst-case Space: O(81) for candidate state; O(81 * recursion_depth) for backtracking \"\"\" # Initialise: all 9 candidates for every cell (bits 0-8 set) all_candidates = (1 << 9) - 1 # 0b111111111 cands = [all_candidates] * 81 # Apply given clues — each triggers propagation for r in range(9): for c in range(9): if board[r][c] != '.': digit = int(board[r][c]) - 1 # 0-indexed if not assign(cands, r * 9 + c, digit): raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution (contradiction in clues)\") result = search(cands) if result is None: raise ValueError(\"Puzzle has no solution\") # Write back: single set bit in result[cell] → the solved digit for c in range(81): board[c // 9][c % 9] = str(result[c].bit_length()) # ── Visualisation helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── def print_board(board: list[list[str]]) -> None: for r, row in enumerate(board): if r in (3, 6): print(\"------+-------+------\") line = \" \".join( cell + (\" |\" if c in (2, 5) else \"\") for c, cell in enumerate(row) ) print(line) # ── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── if __name__ == \"__main__\": # Inkala's \"World's Hardest Sudoku\" (2010) inkala = [ ['8','.','.', '.','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','3', '6','.','.', '.','.', '.'], ['.','7','.', '.','9','.', '2','.', '.'], ['.','5','.', '.','.','7', '.','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','4','5', '7','.', '.'], ['.','.','.','.','1','.', '.','.', '3'], ['.','.','1', '.','.','.', '.','6', '8'], ['.','.','8', '5','.','.', '.','1', '.'], ['.','9','.', '.','.','.', '4','.', '.'], ] solve_sudoku(inkala) print(\"Inkala solution:\") print_board(inkala) # First row should be: 8 1 2 7 5 3 6 4 9 # LeetCode example lc = [ ['5','3','.','.','7','.','.','.','.'], ['6','.','.','1','9','5','.','.','.'], ['.','9','8','.','.','.','.','6','.'], ['8','.','.','.','6','.','.','.','3'], ['4','.','.','8','.','3','.','.','1'], ['7','.','.','.','2','.','.','.','6'], ['.','6','.','.','.','.','2','8','.'], ['.','.','.','4','1','9','.','.','5'], ['.','.','.','.','8','.','.','7','9'], ] solve_sudoku(lc) print(\"\\nLeetCode example solution:\") print_board(lc) ``` --- ## 6. Visual Explanation ### Diagram 1: The Three Constraint Groups for Cell (4,4) ``` 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 ← columns row 0 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 1 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 2 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 3 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 4 [ C C C B X B C C C ] ← X = cell (4,4) row 5 [ . . . B B B . . . ] row 6 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 7 [ . . . . R . . . . ] row 8 [ . . . . R . . . . ] R = row peers (8 cells) → row_unit = {(4,0)..(4,8)} \\ {(4,4)} C = column peers (8 cells) → col_unit = {(0,4)..(8,4)} \\ {(4,4)} B = box peers (8 cells, some overlap with R/C already) → box_unit = {(3,3)..(5,5)} \\ {(4,4)} Unique peers (after dedup) = 20 cells Cell (4,4) eliminates a digit from all 20 simultaneously when assigned. ``` ### Diagram 2: Constraint Propagation in Action ``` Initial board state (partial): Row 0 partially filled. Cell (0,2) has candidates {1,3,5}. Step 1 — Assign digit 5 to cell (0,7): Propagate: eliminate 5 from all 20 peers of (0,7). → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 5. candidates become {1,3}. Step 2 — Another assignment elsewhere forces digit 3 out of row 0: → Cell (0,2) loses candidate 3. candidates become {1}. → Naked Single triggered! Only 1 candidate remains. Step 3 — Cell (0,2) is now forced: assign digit 1. → Propagate: eliminate 1 from all 20 peers of (0,2). → This may trigger further Naked/Hidden Singles in peers. This cascade — one assignment forces ten more — is why propagation alone solves most \"easy\" and \"medium\" puzzles. BEFORE propagation: 51 empty cells, 9 candidates each = up to 459 candidate-slots AFTER one round: many cells drop to 1-3 candidates. Search tree is tiny. ``` ### Diagram 3: MRV Heuristic — Why Cell Order Matters ``` Backtracking without MRV (reading order — first empty cell): Branch on cell (0,0): 4 candidates → 4 children Branch on cell (0,2): 3 candidates → 3 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 4 × 3 = 12 Backtracking with MRV (most constrained first): Branch on cell (6,5): 2 candidates → 2 children Branch on cell (2,8): 2 candidates → 2 children each ... Total tree nodes at depth 2: up to 2 × 2 = 4 MRV reduces branching factor from average ~3 to average ~2. Over 30 levels of recursion: 3^30 ≈ 200 billion vs 2^30 ≈ 1 billion. In practice, propagation collapses most branches before recursion, reducing depth to ~5-15 for hard puzzles. The key insight: a cell with 2 candidates is a coin flip. A cell with 5 candidates is a 5-way gamble. Attack the coin flip first — wrong guesses fail faster. ``` --- ## 7. Common Variations & Extensions ### 7.1 Larger Sudoku Variants (16×16, 25×25) Standard 9×9 Sudoku uses 9-bit masks (one int). For 16×16, extend to 16-bit masks; for 25×25, use 32-bit integers. The peer/unit precomputation generalises directly: for an N²×N² grid (N=block size), each cell has 3(N²-1) - 2(N-1) unique peers. The bitmask operations remain O(1). The key optimisation change: use `__builtin_popcount` (C++) or `bin(x).count('1')` (Python) instead of iterating candidates. ### 7.2 Sudoku Validity Checker (Not Solver) A common interview variant: \"given a partially filled board, is it valid?\" This requires only the constraint check without propagation or search — O(81) scan checking for duplicates in each row/column/box using sets. See [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] for the set-based duplicate detection pattern. ### 7.3 Exact Cover Formulation (Algorithm X / Dancing Links) Sudoku has a beautiful reduction to **exact cover**: given a universe U and a collection S of subsets, select subsets from S that partition U exactly. Sudoku's universe = {row-digit, col-digit, box-digit, cell constraints}, 324 elements. Algorithm X (Knuth 2000) with Dancing Links solves this in O(1) for most puzzles with a sparse doubly-linked list that enables O(1) \"cover\" and \"uncover\" operations. This is the fastest known exact solver. The connection to [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] is direct: DLX is constraint propagation implemented as pointer manipulation rather than bitmask ops. ### 7.4 Sudoku Generation To generate a valid puzzle: start with a solved board (easy to construct by placing 1-9 randomly in the first row and completing with backtracking), then remove clues one at a time, checking after each removal that the puzzle still has a unique solution. The uniqueness check requires running the solver and confirming the search tree has exactly one leaf. This is the algorithm used by newspaper puzzle generators. ### 7.5 Minimum Clues and Hardness The proven minimum number of clues for a uniquely-solvable 9×9 Sudoku is **17** (proved computationally in 2012 by McGuire, Tugemann, Civario). Puzzles with 17 clues are maximally hard for propagation-based solvers. Understanding *why* they're hard connects to the structure of the constraint graph — sparse clues mean few initial propagations, forcing genuine backtracking search. This is why the Inkala puzzle (above) is considered \"hard\" despite having ~20 clues. --- ## 8. LeetCode Practice Problems ``` LC: 36, 37, 51, 52, 79, 212, 980, 1307 ``` *Covers: Sudoku validity check (36), Sudoku solver (37), N-Queens I/II (51/52) for backtracking fundamentals, Word Search I/II (79/212) for grid backtracking, Unique Paths III (980) for constraint grid traversal, All O(1) Data Structure (1307) for constraint state management.* --- ## 9. CP Contest Application ### Typical Contest Structure **Direct Sudoku problems** appear occasionally in implementation rounds and ICPC regionals, typically as: - \"Solve this specific puzzle\" — pure implementation, any correct solver passes - \"Count valid completions given partial board\" — requires counting, not just finding one solution - \"Minimum clues needed for unique solution\" — research-level, not typical in CP **The constraint propagation pattern** is more broadly useful in: - **Grid CSPs:** Nonogram / Picross solving, Kakuro, Hitori — same propagation loop, different constraint definitions - **Exact cover problems:** Pentomino tiling, N-Queens counting, Latin square counting - **Assignment problems with capacity constraints:** \"Assign students to rooms such that each student gets their preference and no room is over-capacity\" — propagation reduces search dramatically ### Time Limits For 9×9 Sudoku: any correct implementation with MRV + propagation solves in < 1ms. Naive backtracking without propagation may TLE even for 9×9 on difficult inputs. For 16×16 Sudoku (occasionally appears): bitmask propagation is mandatory. Pure hash-set approach will TLE. ### Optimisation Tricks for CP 1. **Pre-allocate peer arrays at compile time** — avoid vector allocation per call: ```cpp constexpr int PEERS[81][20] = { /* precomputed at compile time */ }; ``` 2. **Use int arrays, not vectors, for candidate state** — `vector<int> copy = cands` is O(81), but `int copy[81]; memcpy(copy, cands, 81*sizeof(int))` is 3x faster due to cache effects. 3. **Skip Hidden Single rule for speed on easy puzzles** — Naked Single alone handles 95% of standard Sudoku puzzles. Add Hidden Single only if you know the test cases include hard puzzles. 4. **Iterative deepening instead of recursive backtracking** — avoids stack frame overhead on deep searches; rarely needed for 9×9 but relevant for larger variants. --- *Connections: [[77.Backtracking template — choose, explore, unchoose]] · [[78.N-Queens — the canonical backtracking problem]] · [[80.Pruning strategies — forward checking, bounding functions]] · [[82.Feature subset search as backtracking — ML connection]] · [[22.Anagram detection and group-by-signature patterns]] · [[128.Union-Find — union by rank and path compression]]* ENDOFFILE echo \"Done. $(wc -l < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) lines, $(wc -c < /mnt/user-data/outputs/79.Sudoku-solver—constraint-propagation+backtracking.md) bytes\"``#{1}","lines":[3,9],"size":158,"outlinks":[{"title":"mes","target":"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11647753-understanding-usage-and-length-limits","line":7}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},

